---
// The styles of the game belong to the page: the picture of the game that the page renders on the
// server and the game itself use the same ones, which are sent once
import './components/backbone.css';

import { type GameDefinition } from '@/games/registry';
import GameLayout from '@/layouts/GameLayout.astro';

import Backbone from './components/Backbone';
import OverviewSkeleton from './components/OverviewSkeleton';

interface Props {
  game: GameDefinition;
}

const { game } = Astro.props;
---

<GameLayout game={game}>
  {/* Rendered in the browser only: it reads the address and the levels are fetched when it runs */}
  <Backbone client:only="react">
    <OverviewSkeleton slot="fallback" />
  </Backbone>
  <noscript>
    <p class="text-muted">
      This game needs JavaScript to run. Nothing is sent anywhere, it all happens in your browser.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div slot="rules">
    <p>
      You are the network engineer. Every level is a small network and a list of{' '}
      <strong>flows</strong>: traffic that has to get from one node to another. You decide how each
      flow travels, then run a <strong>stress test</strong> and see how it goes.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Plan:</strong> pick a flow, then click the nodes one after the other from its start
        to its end, or drag across them. A link carries only so much (the width of the line and the
        second number on it); what does not fit is dropped.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Different traffic wants different things:</strong> voice must be fast, a payment
        must not lose a packet, a backup does not care. The list says what each flow wants.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run:</strong> the stress test plays one to three situations (a quiet day, a rush, a
        cut cable). Your score is the mean, and the worst situation counts twice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stars:</strong> one star for beating the shortest path, two for beating the load
        balanced strategy as well, three for coming close to the best plan. You can try as often as
        you like. A level you solved lets you load the best plan.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each chapter adds a rule: splitting a flow over several paths, backup paths, holding traffic
        back, redundancy, and changes of path in time.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The helpers fill in the plan for you (shortest path for all, or balanced). A run with a helper
      earns no stars. Nothing is saved, and nothing leaves your browser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="keys">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves into the map, the arrow keys <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd>
        <kbd>↓</kbd> move to the next node in that direction
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd> adds the node to the path (start at the node marked
        "from")
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Backspace</kbd> takes the last node back, <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels the path
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Z</kbd> undoes, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Y</kbd> redoes
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The text version below the map lists every cable with its load and the path of every flow.
      What happens is announced to screen readers.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="built">
    <p>
      The stress test is a small deterministic model in pure TypeScript with unit tests: queues on
      every link, drops, latency, loss and redundancy, failures with backup paths. The best plan of
      every level is proven by an exact search before the level is shown. The user interface is a
      React component that only draws what the model says.
    </p>
  </div>
</GameLayout>
